    join/it-method{
      it >joining-task
      if: ( $joining-task not= l9 ) then: { /join-of-other-task raise };
      while: { $joining-task pending-children/ object.@ 0 > } do: {
        if: ( l9-ready-queue queue.length 0 = ) then: {
          false $joining-task joining?/ object.!
          false $joining-task paused?/ object.!
          /join-no-runnable-child raise
        };
        true $joining-task joining?/ object.!
        true $joining-task paused?/ object.!
        l9-dispatch-next
      };
      false $joining-task joining?/ object.!
      $joining-task subtasks/ object.@ >completed
      array[] $joining-task subtasks/ object.!
      array[] $joining-task forks/ object.!
      array[] $joining-task failed-children/ object.!
      $completed
    }
    ~~ Explicit early completion of the current actor, not a remote kill.
    ~~ The initial actor's l9-actor-finish continuation is a VM sentinel.
    ~~ long-jump runs scope on-return/finally before reaching task hooks.
    exit/it-method{ >result
      return-if( @done? or @finalizing? or @exiting? )
      if: ( it = l9 ) then: {
        if: ( it = l9-root ) then: { /root-task-cannot-exit raise };
        $result @result!
        true @exiting?!
        /l9-actor-finish long-jump
      } else: {
        /exit-of-other-task raise
      };
    }
    ~~ #2026: Old code had: sleep/it-method{  with milliseconds/ parameters
    sleep/it-method{  with milliseconds/ parameter
    }
  }

  ~~ 2026-11-23 - GPT-6 coding agent under Jean Hugues Noel Robert.
  ~~ Class initialization registers the actual make.task dynamically.
  ~~ Resolve the verb AFTER registration; an early compiled call to the
  ~~ historical stub never produced a real root task.
  _ false false make.task/ as-verb run, drop

initialize-l9

~~ Core Inox adapters: explicit operations instead of JavaScript callable objects.
to boxon?
  .class >kind
  $kind /boxon = $kind /moxon = or

to boxon-cast
  >source make.boxon >target
  if: $source boxon? then: {
    $target make.boxon-forwarder $source .observe drop
  } else: {
    if: ( $source .class /promise = ) then: {
      $target false make.boxon-promise-adapter
      $target true make.boxon-promise-adapter
      $source .then drop
    } else: { $source $target .resolve drop };
  };
  $target

to boxon-normalize
  >source if: $source boxon? then: { $source } else: { $source boxon-cast };

to boxon-collect
  >race? >inputs
  $inputs array.length >count
  $count $race? make.boxon-collection >collection
  $collection .output >output
  if: ( $count 0 = ) then: {
    if: $race? not then: { array[] $output .resolve drop };
  };
  0 >idx
  while: { if: ( $idx $count < ) then: { $output .settled? not } else: { false }; } do: {
    $idx $inputs array.get boxon-normalize >source
    $collection $idx make.boxon-collection-observer
    $source .observe drop
    $idx ++ >idx!
  };
  $output

to boxon-all false boxon-collect
to boxon-race true boxon-collect

~~ #2026: Selective testing explanation and diagnostics were added in 2026.
~~ Selective testing / bypassing full l9 for class hierarchy debug:
~~ The class hierarchy is built by the many `class{ ... }` and `update-all-classes`
~~ inside initialize-l9 (executed at load time of this file).
~~ Bug was observed around promise/class it-method / return-if / extend-class
~~ at l9.nox:330 etc, and "Can't extend a non extensible stack", plus
~~ "Invalid return, not a verb" and parse state asserts (bad eq 0/1).
~~
~~ This was caused by return/return-if/return-unless doing direct pop_ip()
~~ while "with-parameters" or "it" frames were on the control stack (CSP)
~~ from class{ / it-method{ / with etc. The fix (unwind of forget- frames
~~ before pop_ip in the return primitives) allows the hierarchy to build.
~~
~~ To selectively test only parts of the hierarchy:
~~   1. Start from lib/test/minimal-hierarchy.nox (template) or cp lib/l9.nox ...
~~   2. In it, include only the needed top-level supporting defs (with-length etc)
~~      + only the suspect class{ ... } blocks (comment the rest of initialize-l9)
~~      (and keep the supporting to/class words defined above).
~~   3. Make sure it ends with the needed update or direct execution.
~~   4. Run: INOX_WITH_L9=1 INOX_L9_FILE=lib/test/minimal-hierarchy.nox \
~~           node builds/inox.js -e ' "test" out '
~~ This bypasses the full l9.nox while still exercising the class building code.
~~
~~ To rule out memory allocator bugs during the build (use the debug verbs
~~ we added for exactly this):
~~   debug-delay-frees
~~   ... the class{ blocks or whole initialize-l9 ...
~~   debug-flush-frees
~~ If the crash/assert still happens while frees are delayed (no areas were
~~ reclaimed/reused during the build), then it's not an obscure free/malloc bug.
~~ The debug-delay-frees / debug-flush-frees are always-available primitives.
~~
~~ For post-load tests (after hierarchy succeeds), use the test/NAME convention
~~ from smoke.nox (define `to test/my-hier ... ; my-hier/test ` ), run with
~~ INOX_WITH_L9=1 INOX_SMOKE=1 ... and your test file will run after load.
~~ Or simply: INOX_WITH_L9=1 node builds/inox.js your-test.nox
~~ (your .nox runs after l9+stdlib; "bypasses" the bin/inox launcher).
~~
~~ Legacy INOX_TEST=1 forces minimal (no l9) + defaults to lib/test.nox .
~~
